────────────────────────────────────────── Final Decision Document ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Deliberation Decision Document
**Feature Request:** Project managers should be able to see the full history of engagement with a country 
before starting a new mission.

**Termination Reason:** `CONVERGENCE_ALL_ISSUES_RESOLVED_OR_ESCALATED (CRITIC_SIGNALED_CONVERGENCE)` | **Final
Disagreement Score:** `0.0` | **Confidence (Proposer / Critic):** `0.75` / `0.45`

## Executive Summary
V1 adds a read-only Engagement History tab on the Country record. It shows a paginated, filterable timeline 
built from existing Engagement records, found by direct or indirect linkage to the country, with a 'Contact 
now inactive' badge, a contact column labelled as current role, and an always-on caveat that a sparse history 
does not mean there was no contact. The main trade-off argued was how cheaply the Critic's concerns about 
misleading history and data exposure could be met without new data entry, tables or gating. Contact staleness,
incomplete linkage and the panel's staleness cue were resolved (C2, C3, C4) with derived or read-time fixes. 
The pre-mission checkpoint in Create Mission and server-side per-record visibility enforcement are pending a 
human decision on C1, and so is the access-testing success criterion. That decision is whether the existing 
access predicate is the right visibility boundary for aggregated engagement history.

## 1. Agreed Scope
### Included in Scope (v1 MVP)
- Read-only 'Engagement History' tab on the existing Country record, showing a reverse-chronological timeline 
of existing Engagement records for that country (date, type, summary, contact, linked Mission/Project, 
logged-by).
- Basic filters on the timeline over existing fields only: date range, engagement type, and linked 
Mission/Project.
- Each timeline entry shows the contact name, linked from the existing Contact record, under a column labelled
as the contact's current role. A derived 'Contact now inactive' badge is shown when the linked Contact's 
existing status is inactive or departed. The timeline does not backfill historical roles.
- Timeline query matches the A1 definition of linkage: an Engagement is included if it is linked to the 
Country directly, or via its Contact, Mission or Project, with duplicates removed. The query is paginated 
(page size 25) and runs against the existing Engagement table with indexes on the existing link columns 
(country, contact, mission, project, engagement_date). No new tables.
- Static caveat on the history tab and checkpoint panel: 'Shows logged engagements only; an empty or sparse 
history does not mean there was no contact.' This is always displayed, with no data entry and no per-country 
setting.

### Pending Human Decision (relies on an escalated issue)
- Pre-mission checkpoint: when a Project Manager opens the 'Create Mission' flow and selects a Country, show 
the last 5 Engagements the viewer is permitted to see, a derived cue 'Last engagement: N months ago' (computed
from the most recent permitted engagement date), and a link 'View full history' that opens the Country 
Engagement History tab. If the viewer has no permitted engagements, show a neutral 'No engagement history 
available to you' message with no counts or summaries. It is informational only and does not block creation. 
*(waits on C1)*
- Visibility is enforced server-side, per record, on the aggregated history query (tab and checkpoint panel 
alike), reusing the same access predicate that already governs read access to Engagement and Mission records. 
The summary text is covered by the same filter. A viewer sees only engagement records they can already see 
today, including engagements on missions they are not assigned to only if the existing model already allows 
it. No new roles, flags, per-country settings or new assignment-based rules. *(waits on C1)*

### Explicitly Excluded from Scope
- Any new data-entry fields or forms for Regional Coordinators; the history only reads what is already logged
- Mandatory acknowledgement, approval or gating step before a mission can be created
- AI/automated summaries, sentiment or relationship scoring of engagement history
- Historical backfill or cleaning of legacy engagement records; data is shown as-is
- Point-in-time contact role/title history (valid_from/valid_to) or contact-name-at-time snapshot columns; 
current contact details plus an inactive badge are shown only
- Cross-country or regional aggregate views and exports (CSV/PDF)
- Per-record or per-country sensitivity flags and custom RBAC; any change to data classification is a human 
policy decision
- Alerts or notifications about stale or missing engagement history
- Editing engagement records from the history view
- Significance ranking, importance flags or filtering of routine logs for the 'last 5' panel
- New assignment-based visibility rules for engagement history; who may see which engagements is a human 
access-policy decision

### Success Criteria
- For 100% of countries, the Country Engagement History tab loads the first page (25 entries) in under 2 
seconds at p95 on production-scale data, using the multi-link query.
- When a Project Manager selects a country in the Create Mission flow, the last 5 permitted engagements and 
the 'Last engagement: N months ago' cue are shown without extra clicks, and 'View full history' is reachable 
in one click.
- Zero new required fields or forms are added for Regional Coordinators, verified by schema and UI review 
before release.
- Within 60 days of release, at least 60% of newly created missions have the country's history tab or panel 
viewed in the same session, measured via view-event logging.
- A pre-release check on production data reports the count of Engagement records included only via 
Contact/Mission/Project linkage (no direct country link) and the count that resolve to no country at all. The 
history query returns 100% of the records that resolve to a country by any A1 path in a fixture test.
- In fixture tests, 100% of timeline entries whose linked Contact has inactive or departed status display the 
'Contact now inactive' badge, and 0 entries display it otherwise.
- *Pending:* In access testing, 0 engagement records (including summary text) are visible to a Project Manager
through the history tab or the Create Mission panel that are not visible to them through the existing 
Engagement/Mission read path. The test suite includes a cross-mission case (PM assigned to Mission A cannot 
see Mission B engagements where the existing model denies it) and a case of selecting a country the PM has no 
access to. *(waits on C1)*

## 2. Surfaced Assumptions & Deliberation Outcomes
- **[A1] 'Full history' means all Engagement records already linked to the Country (directly or via its 
Contacts, Missions and Projects), not a new history log or audit trail.** (Challenged: `False` -> Disposition:
`ACCEPTED`)
  - *Notes:* No issue challenged this assumption, so it was accepted as stated. C3 later required the query to
follow its definition of linkage.
- **[A2] Existing engagement data is sufficiently complete and consistently linked to Country that a timeline 
is useful as-is; gaps are shown, not fixed.** (Challenged: `True` -> Disposition: `REVISED_AND_ACCEPTED`)
  - *Notes:* C3 challenged data completeness and linkage. It was resolved with a multi-link query, an 
always-on caveat and a pre-release data check, and the Critic accepted it, so the assumption was revised and 
accepted.
- **[A3] Project Managers need the history surfaced at the moment they start a mission (a summary in the 
create-mission flow), and informing them is enough; no gating or sign-off is required.** (Challenged: `True` 
-> Disposition: `REVISED_AND_ACCEPTED`)
  - *Notes:* C4 challenged the checkpoint's usefulness and exposure. The Proposer added the access filter and 
a 'Last engagement: N months ago' cue, defended the exclusion of significance ranking, and the Critic 
accepted, so the assumption was revised and accepted. The checkpoint's inclusion remains pending C1.
- **[A4] Project Managers' current read access to Country, Mission and Engagement records is the right 
visibility boundary for this feature; whether any engagement content needs tighter classification is a policy 
question outside this MVP.** (Challenged: `True` -> Disposition: `ESCALATED_TO_HUMAN`)
  - *Notes:* C1 challenged whether the existing access model is the right visibility boundary. The Proposer 
committed to applying the existing per-record predicate server-side, and the Critic escalated the policy 
question to a human, so the assumption is escalated to human.
- **[A5] Showing the contact's current name/role/status from the Contact record is acceptable for history 
entries, even if the role has changed since the engagement.** (Challenged: `True` -> Disposition: 
`REVISED_AND_ACCEPTED`)
  - *Notes:* C2 challenged showing current contact details on historical entries. The Proposer added a 
'Contact now inactive' badge and a current-role label, and the Critic accepted, so the assumption was revised 
and accepted.

## 3. Open Questions Requiring Human / Policy Sign-Off
- **[C1] Unassigned: product owner to route (likely the security or data-classification owner):** Engineering 
cannot decide this access policy. The history tab and Create Mission panel will reuse the existing per-record 
read predicate for Engagement and Mission records, summary text included, and will add no new assignment-based
rules. Is that predicate an acceptable visibility boundary for an aggregated country history, which could let 
a Project Manager read sensitive summaries from missions they were never assigned to, such as a ministerial 
dispute or back-channel contact? Or must access be restricted further by a policy owned by a human? The answer
determines whether the pre-mission checkpoint, server-side visibility enforcement and the access-testing 
criterion can proceed.
  - *Why it is here:* The Critic escalated it as a policy question outside engineering's authority.

